# Known-accepted CVEs. Every entry MUST carry a dated comment explaining why
# it is ignored and when to re-review. No undated entries. See Plan 064 A2.
#
# `ignore-unfixed: true` on every Trivy scan already excludes every CVE with
# no published fix before either scan can report it — so an entry here can
# never legitimately read "no fix yet". The one case this file is for: a fix
# DOES exist upstream but we cannot adopt it (e.g. it ships in a package
# suite we do not track, or a Python dependency we cannot move yet). See
# docs/standards/security.md § CVE scanning layers for the full policy
# (Plan 180 D2/D3). The re-review date on each entry is a dated COMMENT, not
# an enforced check — nothing currently re-checks it automatically.

# --- No active entries as of 2026-06-02 ---
# The base image was re-pinned to the python:3.12.13-slim debian 13.5 rebuild
# (digest sha256:090ba77e…) on 2026-06-02. That rebuild cleared all previously
# accepted CVEs, so they were removed rather than re-dated:
#   - OpenSSL (libssl3t64/openssl/openssl-provider-legacy): CVE-2026-28390,
#     -28388, -28389, -31789, -31790, -2673, -28387 — fixed in debian 13.5.
#   - pip 25.0.1: CVE-2025-8869, CVE-2026-1703, CVE-2026-6357 — no longer
#     flagged HIGH/CRITICAL by the current Trivy DB for the bundled pip.
#   - libcap2 CVE-2026-4878 (the finding that turned CI red on 2026-06-01) is
#     fixed in libcap2 1:2.75-10+deb13u1, present in the 13.5 rebuild.
# A fresh `trivy image` scan of the rebuilt base reports 0 HIGH/CRITICAL.
# Re-populate this file (with dated comments) only when a future scan surfaces
# a HIGH/CRITICAL CVE that has a published fix we cannot adopt.
